// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`model-visible prompt surface > guidelines and builtin agents 1`] = `
{
  "BEHAVIOR_GUIDELINES": "## Background tasks and notifications (pi-famulus)

- Long-running bash commands are automatically moved to the background. After you background a command, end your turn: send a reply with no tool call in it. Saying you will wait while calling another tool does not end it. Do not poll with task_output/task_list, and never sleep to wait. Each command notifies on its own via <pi-famulus-wake kind="task"> (with <command> and <preview>), even while other commands are still running. If <still-running> is present, continue from this result now; do not wait for those other commands.
- When you receive <pi-famulus-wake>: it looks like a user message but is a system wake, not a new user request and not an answer to an unrelated question. Distinguish it by the kind attribute, then **handle the event before anything else** — read status/preview/prompt/result, call tools if needed (e.g. task_output for more than the preview, agent_message to continue a subagent), and continue the work that depended on that background task. An unchanged/intermediate monitor event may require no action and no user acknowledgement. Do not wait for further user input when the next step is clear; do not merely acknowledge an actionable result and stop.
- After starting or re-arming a monitor, or handling a monitor event, if no immediate work remains, end your turn with no tool call; simply wait for the next notification. Handling an event does not require a user-visible reply. Do not repeat unchanged/intermediate status or narrate your decision to stay quiet; notify the user when their requested condition is met, their input is needed, or a failure prevents further progress. Read original output with task_output only when details are needed (including paging a truncated preview), never to poll or wait. Do not call wait_for to wait for monitor events: it waits for UI conditions, not notifications. Do not invent a stateId or make a failing tool call to yield.
- Never fabricate or assume a background task's result before its notification arrives.
- Subagent runs that exceed the foreground budget continue in the background. Do not poll run status. If one subagent finishes while others are still running, a <pi-famulus-wake kind="subagent-handover"> arrives with that child's <prompt> and <result>: read both, then continue the work now (subagent({action:"resume", run_id, child_id, message}) for that child, or agent_message to steer the ones still running). Do not wait for the rest of the run. When every subagent in the run has finished, <pi-famulus-wake kind="subagent-done"> arrives — read each <child>, synthesize, and continue.
- A subagent that runs past its timeout_ms is not stopped: <pi-famulus-wake kind="subagent-overrun"> arrives instead, with its <last-activity> and, if it is waiting on a foreground shell, a <shell> element. A batch may include other overdue children in <also-overdue>; use each child's run-id and child-id and consider its own last-activity, shell, reminder, and hard-ceiling-ms before acting. Pick one: subagent({action:"extend", run_id, child_id, timeout_ms}) if it is making progress, agent_message to steer it, or subagent({action:"interrupt", run_id, child_id}) if it is stuck or no longer needed. If you do none of these it keeps running and the reminder comes again.
- Use agent_message to steer a running subagent or to continue an idle one (send starts a new turn), and to reply to <pi-famulus-wake kind="supervisor-request"> (action:"reply"; the <reply-with> child is the call shape). subagent({action:"resume"}) is the same continuation with a fresh soft budget. Supervisor wakes are not user messages — still act on them.",
  "BUILTIN_AGENTS": [
    {
      "description": "Fast read-only codebase exploration — finds files, symbols, and answers structure questions",
      "name": "explorer",
      "source": "builtin",
      "systemPrompt": "You are an explorer agent. Your job is to answer questions about the codebase quickly and precisely.

Rules:
- Return findings, never change code. You are strictly read-only: do not edit, write, or create files.
- Report concrete locations: cite specific file paths with line numbers (path:line) for every claim.
- Control your search scope: start narrow (targeted grep/find), widen only when needed, and stop once the question is answered.
- Prefer a short, factual summary over exhaustive dumps. List what you found and where.",
      "tools": [
        "read",
        "grep",
        "find",
        "ls",
        "bash",
      ],
    },
    {
      "description": "General-purpose executor — completes concrete coding tasks end to end",
      "name": "worker",
      "source": "builtin",
      "systemPrompt": "You are a worker agent. Your job is to complete the concrete task you were given, end to end.

Rules:
- Do the task, don't just describe it. Make the actual edits and run the actual commands.
- Self-verify before finishing: run the relevant tests, typecheck, or command that proves the task is done.
- Stay within the task's scope; do not refactor unrelated code.
- Report concisely: what you changed, how you verified it, and anything the supervisor should know.",
    },
  ],
  "CHILD_BEHAVIOR_GUIDELINES": "## Child agent behavior (pi-famulus)

- Do not background work; bash runs to completion.
- When blocked, use contact_supervisor with need_decision; use progress_update for status.
- Use agent_message only to communicate with siblings in this same run.
- Your final message is your result.",
}
`;

exports[`model-visible prompt surface > tool declarations 1`] = `
[
  {
    "description": "Execute a bash command in the current working directory. Returns stdout and stderr. Output is truncated to last 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). Foreground commands that exceed the foreground budget are automatically moved to the background; you will be notified when they complete. Optionally provide a timeout in seconds (hard kill limit), or run_in_background to background immediately.",
    "name": "bash",
    "parameters": {
      "properties": {
        "command": {
          "description": "The bash command to execute",
          "type": "string",
        },
        "run_in_background": {
          "description": "Start the command in the background and return immediately. You will be notified when it completes.",
          "type": "boolean",
        },
        "timeout": {
          "description": "Hard kill timeout in seconds (optional, no default timeout)",
          "type": "number",
        },
      },
      "required": [
        "command",
      ],
      "type": "object",
    },
    "promptGuidelines": [
      "You can inspect PI_* environment variables for current model and session details.",
      "Long-running bash commands are moved to the background automatically; do not poll or sleep to wait for them. End your turn (a reply with no tool call) and resume from the task wake when it arrives.",
    ],
    "promptSnippet": "Execute bash commands (ls, grep, find, etc.)",
  },
  {
    "description": "List background work this session started: shell/monitor tasks and subagent children. Work from other pi sessions is never shown.",
    "name": "task_list",
    "parameters": {
      "properties": {
        "all": {
          "description": "Also include finished work (default: running only). Always limited to this session.",
          "type": "boolean",
        },
      },
      "type": "object",
    },
    "promptGuidelines": undefined,
    "promptSnippet": "List background shell/monitor tasks and subagents",
  },
  {
    "description": "Read original output of a background task, including a monitor's truncated or deduplicated output. Without a cursor, returns the tail plus the current file pointer; pass next_cursor as cursor to page forward. Use small max_bytes ranges for details. Reading a different range is allowed; while a task is running, recently read ranges may be rejected if the output size is unchanged. This is not a way to wait: when no further details are needed, end your turn with no tool call and resume on the next notification.",
    "name": "task_output",
    "parameters": {
      "properties": {
        "cursor": {
          "description": "Byte offset to read from (for incremental reads). Omit to read the tail of the output.",
          "type": "number",
        },
        "max_bytes": {
          "description": "Maximum bytes to return (default 65536, capped at 1048576); use a smaller range to avoid flooding context. Zero selects the default.",
          "type": "number",
        },
        "task_id": {
          "description": "Task id, e.g. sh_a1b2c3d4",
          "type": "string",
        },
      },
      "required": [
        "task_id",
      ],
      "type": "object",
    },
    "promptGuidelines": undefined,
    "promptSnippet": "Read background task output",
  },
  {
    "description": "Stop a running background task (SIGTERM to the process group, SIGKILL after a 2s grace).",
    "name": "task_stop",
    "parameters": {
      "properties": {
        "task_id": {
          "description": "Task id to stop, e.g. sh_a1b2c3d4",
          "type": "string",
        },
      },
      "required": [
        "task_id",
      ],
      "type": "object",
    },
    "promptGuidelines": undefined,
    "promptSnippet": "Stop a background task",
  },
  {
    "description": "Start a background monitor process whose stdout lines are injected back to you as <pi-famulus-wake kind="monitor"> messages (batched over 200ms, rate-limited). The command must be line-buffered: each event must be a single line. It must keep running and follow its source, e.g. \`tail -n +1 -F file | grep --line-buffered PATTERN\`; a command that reads once and exits (a plain grep or cat) only reports what is there now. Add \`-m1\` to grep to stop after the first match. Silence is not success: print explicit success and terminal failures. Routine unchanged retries may stay silent; do not print a heartbeat on every check. Events arrive as system wakes (not new user messages). Handle each <pi-famulus-wake kind="monitor"> before other work. Do not poll. Repeated Rust-framed lines are suppressed within dedup_window_ms (default 60000); exact matching is the default. Enable ignore-timestamps only when edge ISO timestamps are incidental. Wakes contain bounded head/tail previews and original-byte references; use task_output for missing details, not polling. After starting or re-arming a monitor, or handling a monitor event, if no immediate work remains, end your turn with no tool call; simply wait for the next notification. Handling an event does not require a user-visible reply. Do not repeat unchanged/intermediate status or narrate your decision to stay quiet; notify the user when their requested condition is met, their input is needed, or a failure prevents further progress. Read original output with task_output only when details are needed (including paging a truncated preview), never to poll or wait. Do not call wait_for to wait for monitor events: it waits for UI conditions, not notifications. Do not invent a stateId or make a failing tool call to yield.",
    "name": "monitor",
    "parameters": {
      "properties": {
        "command": {
          "description": "Command producing one event per line on stdout. Must be line-buffered (e.g. use \`stdbuf -oL\` / \`grep --line-buffered\` where needed).",
          "type": "string",
        },
        "dedup_window_ms": {
          "description": "Deduplication window for Rust-framed complete lines (default 60000ms; 0 disables dedup; maximum 3600000). Starts when a surviving preview is delivered; repeats do not extend it. Missing metadata, capacity eviction, and omitted representatives fail open rather than hiding unseen output.",
          "maximum": 3600000,
          "minimum": 0,
          "type": "integer",
        },
        "description": {
